# Chroma library, modern Earth: ELDER stage (ages about 60 and over). Grid: earth-plan.md. Rules: library-spec.md.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Compile and check the whole batch: python3 build.py earth

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 1: calls WU (cycle s2) / calls BRG (cycle s4)

== learning to use a new phone
tier: everyday
stages: elder
age: 60-105
calls: WU
tone: mixed
life: era, everyday
horizon: week
roles: child, grandchild, friend, a shop assistant
earth: your daughter gives you a smartphone and sets it up for video calls
times: Most older people, a new phone every few years; most Americans over 65 own a smartphone (Pew 2021: 61 in 100)
gap: 2-5
scene: {child} has set up a new phone for {N}, all glass and no buttons, and it buzzes in {Ns} palm like a trapped bee.
scene W: {child} has written the steps on a card in neat capitals. {N} means to follow them in order, the way instructions are meant to be followed.
scene U: The phone has a hundred settings and no printed manual. {N} puts on reading glasses and decides to understand every one of them.
scene B: With this thing {N} could check the bank, the pension and the price of everything without asking anyone for help ever again.
scene R: A video of the grandchildren arrives with a ping, and {N} laughs out loud at the kitchen table and wants more of them.
scene G: {N} turns the phone over in hands that learned on a dial telephone, and wonders whether a life needs this at all.
- W | go to the library's free class for older learners and do the homework | door | v: conformity, self-direction | chance: 85
- U | work through every setting yourself, and quietly turn off anything that reports on you | v: self-direction, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: the family's peace of mind; backfire: found out, and a row | act: work through every setting themselves, and quietly turn off anything that reports on them | chance: 65
- B | learn just enough to run your bank and pension without anyone looking over your shoulder | v: self-direction, power | chance: 75
- R | press everything, send odd messages to everyone, and laugh at the mistakes | door | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 55
- G | put it in the drawer and keep the old phone that still works | v: tradition | chance: 86
- W>B | take it to the shop and insist on the free help their advert promised | v: power, security | chance: 60
- U>R | learn to film and send silly videos to the grandchildren | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 75
- B>G | pretend you cannot learn it, so the children keep coming round to help | habit | v: security, benevolence | chance: 75
- R>W | join the street's group chat the very first evening, and greet everyone | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 75
- G>U | learn it slowly, one thing a week, the way you once learned to knit | v: self-direction | chance: 80
works: By the end of the week {N} sends a photograph to {child} without any help.
works: The first video call works, and a small face fills the screen.
fails: The phone locks itself after three wrong codes and stays locked.
fails: {N} deletes all the contacts by accident and has to ask for help again.

== a fall at home
tier: everyday
at: hospital
stages: elder
age: 65-110
calls: BRG
tone: trouble
life: body, home
horizon: week
roles: child, neighbour, partner, a paramedic, a physiotherapist
earth: your foot catches the hall rug and you go down hard
times: About 1 in 4 people over 65 fall each year, and one fall doubles the chance of another (CDC)
gap: 2-4
scene: {Ns} foot catches the hall rug, and suddenly the ceiling is very far away and the floor is very cold.
scene W: {N} lies on the hall floor reciting what the leaflet said to do after a fall: keep still, check for pain, call for help.
scene U: From the floor {N} works out exactly what happened: the curled rug, the dim bulb, the reading glasses left upstairs.
scene B: Flat on the floor, {Ns} first thought is that if {child} hears of this, the talk about moving will start all over again.
scene R: {N} is down on the hallway floor, furious at the rug, at the knee, at being old at all.
scene G: Lying on the cold tiles, {N} listens to the clock and the rain, and waits for the body to say what it can do.
- W | phone for help at once, as you promised the family, and wait | v: security, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 90
- U | work out why you fell and make the house safe, room by room | v: security, self-direction | chance: 80
- B | get up, clean up, and tell nobody, so nobody can say you cannot cope | v: power, self-direction | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 58
- R | haul yourself up, swear at the rug and throw it out the back door | body: heavy | v: none clear (feeling let out) | chance: 54
- G | lie still, breathe, and let your body tell you when it can move | v: security, tradition (accepting limits) | act: lie still, breathe, and wait until their body says it can move | chance: 70
- W>G | sign up for the clinic's balance class, so you can keep your garden | habit | v: security, tradition | chance: 78
- U>W | read up on falls and run a safety check for the neighbours too | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 78
- B>U | hire a private physiotherapist and rebuild your strength by the numbers | body: light | v: achievement, security | chance: 80
- R>B | refuse the walking frame and keep walking to the shops alone | body: heavy | v: self-direction, power | chance: 53
- G>R | walk in the park every morning until your legs and your freedom come back | habit | body: light | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 70
works: The bruise fades, and {N} is walking to the shops again within the month.
works: Nothing is broken, and the house is safer for it.
fails: The hip is cracked, and the weeks in hospital are long.
fails: {N} lies on the floor for hours before anyone comes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 2: calls UB (cycle s3) / calls WRG (cycle s1)

== a scam phone call
tier: everyday
at: bank
stages: elder
age: 60-105
calls: UB
tone: trouble
life: money
horizon: moment
roles: a stranger on the phone, child, friend, the bank
earth: a polite voice says it is your bank, and your savings must be moved tonight
times: Nearly every older person gets scam calls; a convincing one that names your bank comes every few years (estimate)
gap: 2-4
scene: The phone rings at dinner. A calm voice says it is the bank's fraud team, and that {Ns} savings must be moved tonight.
scene W: The caller quotes a reference number and speaks for the bank. {N} was raised to trust people who speak for an institution.
scene U: The caller knows {Ns} name and the last four digits of the card. {N} listens closely for the one thing that does not fit.
scene B: Someone is trying to play {N} for a fool over the phone. {N} smiles at the kitchen wall: two can play at that.
scene R: The voice is so urgent that {Ns} heart starts racing. The money, the money, and no time to think.
scene G: {N} remembers when the bank was a man called by his first name behind a counter in {place}. This voice is nobody {N} knows.
- W | hang up and ring the bank on the number printed on your card | v: security, conformity | chance: 95
- U | keep them talking and ask questions only the real bank could answer | v: self-direction, security | chance: 70
- B | pretend to fall for it and feed them a made-up account number | v: power, stimulation | chance: 70
- R | tell the caller exactly what you think of them and slam the phone down | v: none clear (feeling let out) | chance: 92
- G | trust your gut, say you will sort it out at your branch, and hang up | v: security, tradition | chance: 92
- W>R | tape a rule by the phone, never talk money on a call, and stop dreading it | v: security, self-direction | chance: 90
- U>G | look the scam up and show your friends and family how it works | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 90
- B>W | play along and set them up for the police to catch | v: universalism, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 18
- R>U | ring the number straight back to find out who on earth they are | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 20
- G>B | draw your savings out and keep them at home in a tin, as your parents did | v: security, tradition | chance: 60
works: The call ends, the money stays where it is, and {N} sleeps soundly.
works: The bank thanks {N} and blocks the number for good.
fails: By morning, money is missing from the account.
fails: {N} is shaken for weeks and jumps every time the phone rings.

== a grandchild's visit
tier: everyday
stages: elder
age: 55-100
calls: WRG
tone: joy
life: family
horizon: week
roles: grandchild, child, partner
earth: your grandchildren come to stay for the half-term week
times: Most grandparents, a few times a year in the school holidays (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: {child} drops the grandchildren off for the week, and the quiet house fills with shoes, crumbs and questions.
scene W: {N} has made up the beds with fresh sheets and written out the week: meals at set times, bed at eight, church on Sunday.
scene U: The questions come at the rate of one a minute, and {N} wants to answer every single one of them properly.
scene B: {child} warns that the little ones are a handful. {N} has managed far harder cases and means to show how it is done.
scene R: The grandchildren tumble through the door shrieking, and {N} feels sixty years drop away in one hug.
scene G: The youngest has {Ns} mother's chin and {Ns} own stubborn frown. The line goes on.
- W | hold the grandchildren to your house rules, whatever their parents allow | v: conformity, tradition | closed: approval: their parents object; backfire: the children beg not to stay again | chance: 55
- U | take them to the science museum and answer every question | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 80
- B | spoil them rotten so you are their favourite, not the other grandparents | v: power, hedonism | chance: 90
- R | build a den in the living room and start a pillow fight | body: light | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 85
- G | take them to the woods to look for mushrooms and frogspawn, as you did | v: tradition, universalism (nature) | chance: 70
- W>U | set a reading hour every afternoon and keep to it all week | v: conformity, self-direction | chance: 60
- U>B | teach them chess properly and never once let them win | v: achievement | chance: 45
- B>R | bribe them off their screens with coins, then race them to the river | body: light | v: stimulation | chance: 75
- R>G | dance with them in the kitchen to the songs your mother sang | v: tradition, hedonism | chance: 83
- G>W | cook the big Sunday lunch together and seat the whole family round it | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 75
works: When {child} comes to collect them, nobody wants to leave.
works: The house is a wreck, and {N} has not laughed so much in years.
fails: By Wednesday {N} is worn out and the little ones are bored and fractious.
fails: There is a tearful phone call home, and the visit ends early.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 3: calls BR (cycle s4) / calls WUG (cycle s2)

== a birthday party in your honour
tier: everyday
stages: elder
age: 60-110
calls: BR
tone: joy
life: family
horizon: moment
roles: child, sibling, friend, grandchild, partner
earth: the family hires a hall for your eightieth
times: Most older people, at round birthdays: 70, 80, 90 (estimate)
gap: 8-12
scene: There is a banner across the hall, a cake with far too many candles, and everyone {N} loves in one room.
scene W: {N} stands at the head of the table before three generations who came because {N} matters to them. A speech is expected.
scene U: {N} studies the faces one by one, matching each to a year, a place and a story, like a puzzle finally solved.
scene B: Tonight {N} is the centre of the room, and everyone wants a minute. {N} means to enjoy every second of being the one who counts.
scene R: The band starts an old song, and {Ns} feet are moving before {N} has decided anything.
scene G: Eighty candles. {N} thinks of the oak in the yard at {place}, ringed one year at a time.
- W | give a proper speech and thank every guest by name | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 90
- U | ask each guest for one memory and write them all in a book | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 90
- B | hold court at the top table and let the guests come to you | v: power | act: hold court at the top table and let the guests come to them | chance: 80
- R | dance with everyone until the band packs up | body: heavy | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 64
- G | sit back and watch the children run around the tables, content | v: hedonism (quiet pleasure), benevolence | chance: 90
- W>G | raise a toast to the ones who are no longer here | v: tradition | chance: 92
- U>W | plan the seating so the feuding cousins never share a table | v: benevolence, security | chance: 70
- B>U | ask for money instead of presents and spend it on the course you always wanted | door | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- R>B | stand on a chair and announce you are spending the inheritance on yourself | identity | v: hedonism, power | chance: 45
- G>R | kick off your shoes and dance barefoot on the lawn, as you did when young | body: light | v: hedonism, tradition | chance: 75
works: The photographs from that night stay on every fridge in the family.
works: {N} goes to bed tired, full and very loved.
fails: An old quarrel flares at the table, and the party ends early.
fails: {N} spends the evening feeling like an exhibit, smiling for people barely known.

== the doctor says to stop driving
tier: everyday
at: hospital
stages: elder
age: 68-105
calls: WUG
tone: trouble
life: body, freedom
horizon: months
roles: a doctor, child, friend, neighbour
earth: after the eye test the doctor says your licence should go
times: Perhaps 1 in 3 older drivers hear it from a doctor, once (estimate); US men outlive their driving by about 6 years, women by about 10 (Foley and colleagues 2002)
once: yes
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: The doctor taps the eye chart and says, gently, that it is time to give up the car.
scene W: The rules are clear and the doctor has signed the form. {N} has never once driven without a valid licence.
scene U: {N} asks to see the test results and wants to know exactly which line of the chart was missed, and why.
scene B: Without the car {N} will have to ask {child} for every lift, and {N} hates owing anyone anything.
scene R: The car is the last thing that lets {N} simply go: to the coast, to {friend}, anywhere at all, on a whim.
scene G: {N} has driven the lanes around {place} for fifty years and knows every bend by heart, if no longer by sight.
- W | hand in the licence the same week, as the rules require | v: conformity | chance: 70
- U | learn the eye chart by heart before the retest | v: self-direction | closed: law: cheating a medical test; backfire: caught, and the licence gone for good | chance: 40
- B | keep driving quietly, only by day and only to the shops | habit | v: self-direction, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: driving against medical advice; backfire: an accident or a fine | chance: 75
- R | take the car out for one last long drive to the sea | v: stimulation, hedonism | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 65
- G | give the car to a young relative and walk wherever your legs allow | body: light | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 65
- W>B | petition the council for a bus stop at the end of your road | v: power, security | chance: 25
- U>R | learn the bus and train timetables, and plan trips beyond anywhere the car went | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 80
- B>G | trade your car to the neighbour's son in return for weekly lifts | binds | v: security, tradition | chance: 70
- R>W | rage about it, then throw yourself into the campaign for better buses | v: universalism, benevolence | tags: commit:community | chance: 65
- G>U | accept it, and use the slower days for the books you never had time for | door | v: self-direction | chance: 78
works: Within a month {N} knows the bus drivers by name.
works: {N} finds that life still reaches as far as it needs to.
fails: The house feels smaller every week without the car.
fails: {N} misses {friend}'s birthday for want of a lift, and broods for days.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 4: calls RG (cycle s1) / calls WUB (cycle s3)

== a sunny day in the park
tier: everyday
stages: elder
age: 60-110
calls: RG
tone: joy
life: leisure, nature
horizon: moment
roles: friend, partner, dog walkers, strangers
earth: the first warm day of spring, and the bench by the pond is free
times: Most older people, many fine days a year; a few each season stand out (estimate)
gap: 0-0.5
scene: The first warm day of the year. The park smells of cut grass, and the bench by the pond is free.
scene W: {N} brings a flask and a cushion, and nods to the same dog walkers who pass at the same hour every day.
scene U: {N} counts eleven kinds of bird from the bench and wishes for a better field guide.
scene B: The best bench, the one in full sun with a view of the whole pond, is empty. {N} means to have it before anyone else does.
scene R: The sun is warm on {Ns} face, an ice-cream van is playing its tune, and {N} feels nineteen.
scene G: {N} sits under the chestnut that was a sapling when {N} was a child, one hand flat on the warm bark.
- W | pick up the litter around the bench before sitting down | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 95
- U | bring the field guide and log every bird you see | habit | v: self-direction, universalism (nature) | mark: learned a skill | chance: 93
- B | claim the sunniest bench and spread out so nobody else sits there | habit | v: power, hedonism | chance: 90
- R | buy the biggest ice cream and paddle in the fountain | body: light | v: hedonism, stimulation | closed: approval: no paddling allowed; backfire: told off by the park keeper | chance: 85
- G | feed the ducks bread as you always have, whatever the new signs say | v: tradition, hedonism | closed: approval: the signs ask people not to; backfire: a telling-off from the warden | chance: 85
- W>U | join the guided nature walk the park runs every Saturday | door | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 88
- U>B | watch the chess players by the fountain for a week, then beat their champion | v: achievement | chance: 25
- B>R | bet your friend you can still beat them to the bandstand, and run | body: heavy | v: stimulation | chance: 50
- R>G | climb the old oak you climbed as a child, at least to the first branch | body: heavy | v: stimulation, tradition | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 25
- G>W | share your bench and your sandwiches with whoever sits down | door | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 65
works: {N} comes home with a sunburnt nose, humming.
works: The afternoon turns into a long talk with a stranger who becomes a friend.
fails: The sun goes in, the wind gets up, and {N} catches a chill.
fails: A sudden shower soaks {N} to the skin on the long walk home.

== sorting out your will
tier: everyday
stages: elder
age: 60-105
calls: WUB
tone: mixed
life: money, family
horizon: months
roles: a solicitor, child, sibling, grandchild, partner
earth: the solicitor writes that your will is twenty years out of date
times: About half of older people, once or twice; only about 1 in 3 US adults has a will at all (Caring.com survey 2024)
gap: 8-15
share: .5
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: The old will is in a brown envelope from twenty years ago. Half the people in it have changed, and two of them have died.
scene W: {N} wants it fair, legal and properly witnessed, so that nobody can quarrel over it at the funeral.
scene U: {N} spreads the accounts, the deeds and the policies across the dining table and draws the whole estate as a diagram.
scene B: Everyone in the family will be a little kinder to {N} once they hear a will is being written. {N} knows that very well.
scene R: {N} cannot bear the thought of {child} and {sibling} fighting over the furniture when it is all over.
scene G: The ring was {Ns} grandmother's, and the clock came from the farm. Things should go where they belong.
- W | divide everything fairly between your heirs, signed and witnessed properly | v: universalism (fairness), conformity | chance: 88
- U | quietly move money between accounts so nobody can follow it | v: security, power | closed: law: hiding assets from the tax office; backfire: an inquiry and frozen accounts | chance: 60
- B | leave the most to whoever has done the most, and say so plainly | v: power | chance: 75
- R | give the treasured things away now, while you can see their faces | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 80
- G | leave each heirloom to the one it belongs with, as your family always has | v: tradition | chance: 72
- W>R | write a formal letter of wishes: no mourning clothes, a party, and your favourite songs | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 94
- U>G | set up a careful trust so what came down to you stays in the family | binds | v: tradition, security | act: set up a careful trust so what came down to them stays in the family | chance: 75
- B>W | hold off signing until everyone in the family is on speaking terms | v: benevolence, power | chance: 40
- R>U | follow your heart and leave the most to the young relative who longs to study | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 65
- G>B | keep everything in your own name to the end; the young can wait their turn | v: power, security | chance: 75
works: The will is signed, witnessed and filed, and a weight lifts from {Ns} shoulders.
works: Over Sunday lunch the family takes the plan far better than {N} feared.
fails: The papers sit unsigned on the sideboard for another year.
fails: Word gets out, and {child} stops speaking to {sibling}.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 5: calls WG (cycle s2) / calls UBR (cycle s4)

== a memorial service for a friend
tier: everyday
at: faith body
stages: elder
age: 60-110
calls: WG
tone: trouble
life: loss, faith
horizon: week
roles: dead, friend, rival, the friend's family, a priest
earth: the church is full for your oldest friend's memorial service
times: Most people over 70, about once a year, more often after 80 (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: The order of service has {dead} on the front, younger than {N} remembers. The church is full to the doors.
scene W: The family has asked {N} to read the second lesson, and {N} has practised it twenty times. It must be done right, for {dead}.
scene U: {N} reads the dates on the card and does the sum: {dead} is the third of the old crowd to go this year.
scene B: The family has asked {rival}, not {N}, to give the eulogy. {N} knew {dead} far better, and sits three rows back.
scene R: The organ starts, and {N} is crying before the first hymn, and does not care who sees.
scene G: Outside the church the cherry is in blossom, as it was the spring {dead} married. Everything comes round.
- W | read the lesson clearly and steadily, as you promised the family | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: kept your word | chance: 78
- U | piece together, from old letters, the parts of their life you never knew | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B | work the reception and make yourself useful to the people who matter | v: power | chance: 75
- R | stand up uninvited and tell the funniest story about them you know | v: stimulation, benevolence | closed: approval: not on the order of service; backfire: the family is offended | chance: 70
- G | sit quietly at the back and let the old hymns carry you | v: tradition | tags: commit:faith | act: sit quietly at the back and let the old hymns carry them | chance: 87
- W>B | offer to run the reception, so everyone sees who holds things together | v: power, conformity | chance: 75
- U>R | make a slideshow of their wildest adventures, set to their favourite song | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 70
- B>G | quietly pay for the wake, so the old crowd can stay together all evening | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 90
- R>W | start the old songs at the wake until the whole room joins in | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 78
- G>U | walk down to the river afterwards and think about what dying is | v: self-direction | chance: 85
works: The service is everything {dead} would have wanted, and {N} tells the family so.
works: {N} walks home in the evening light, sad and somehow lighter.
fails: {Ns} voice breaks halfway through, and the words will not come.
fails: {N} goes home to a quiet house and lies awake until dawn.

== your children want to decide for you
tier: everyday
stages: elder
age: 70-110
calls: UBR
tone: trouble
life: family
horizon: months
roles: child, sibling, a doctor, a social worker
earth: your children hold a meeting about your future, without you
times: Many older parents, once or twice in late life; about 7 in 10 people turning 65 will need some long-term care (US Department of Health and Human Services)
gap: 6-12
scene: {child} has called a family meeting about {Ns} future, and {N} only found out because the agenda was left by the printer.
scene W: {child} means well and has a folder of care brochures. {N} was raised to respect family decisions, but this is {Ns} own life.
scene U: {N} reads every line of the brochures {child} left behind, looking for what they actually think is wrong.
scene B: They want power of attorney. {N} has not signed a paper in sixty years without reading it, and is not about to start.
scene R: {N} is being talked about like a parcel to be posted. The anger comes up hot and fast.
scene G: Forty years ago {N} sat {Ns} own mother down for this very talk. Now the wheel has turned.
- W | accept the doctor's assessment and abide by whatever it says | v: conformity, security | mark: gave in to pressure | self_control: - | chance: 60
- U | learn your rights and arrive with the facts, the costs and a plan of your own | v: self-direction, security | chance: 70
- B | arrange your own help at home, your way, before they arrange it | v: self-direction, power | chance: 65
- R | tell them flatly it is your life, and walk out of the meeting | v: self-direction | chance: 54
- G | let them decide; they are family, and it is their turn now | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: gave in to pressure | self_control: - | chance: 55
- W>G | ask an old family friend everyone respects to chair a proper family council | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 70
- U>W | write your wishes down in an advance directive, so there are rules to follow | binds | v: security, conformity | chance: 88
- B>U | secretly record the meeting to learn what each of them really wants | v: security, self-direction | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a secret recording; backfire: found out, and trust is gone | chance: 80
- R>B | rush to the bank next morning and put everything in your sole name | v: power, security | chance: 65
- G>R | go off to an old friend's in the country for a month and let them stew | v: self-direction | chance: 40
works: The family settles on a plan that leaves {N} in charge of {Ns} own days.
works: {child} apologises, and the brochures go out with the recycling.
fails: The decision is made over {Ns} head, and {N} feels like a guest in a borrowed life.
fails: A hard silence settles between {N} and {child} and lasts for months.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 6: calls WB (cycle s3) / calls URG (cycle s1)

== a seat on the residents' committee
tier: everyday
at: council
stages: elder
age: 60-100
calls: WB
tone: mixed
life: community
horizon: months
roles: neighbour, rival, friend, the housing manager
earth: the residents' committee of your block of flats has a seat free
times: About 1 in 3 older people, a few times, see a committee seat open; fewer stand for it (estimate)
gap: 6-12
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: A notice on the board in the lobby: the residents' committee needs a new member, and the vote is on Thursday.
scene W: The lifts have been broken for a month and the bins overflow. Somebody has to take this on properly, and {N} has the time.
scene U: {N} has read the lease, the service-charge accounts and the minutes of the last three meetings, and the numbers do not add up.
scene B: Whoever sits on the committee decides who gets the parking spaces and which contractor is paid. {N} would like that say.
scene R: {rival} has run the committee like a private club for years, and {N} is itching to shake it up.
scene G: {N} has lived in the block longer than anyone and remembers when it was fields. Somebody should speak for the place.
- W | stand for the seat and promise open meetings every month | binds | v: conformity, benevolence | tags: commit:community | chance: 75
- U | dig into the accounts and present what you find at the meeting | identity | v: universalism (fairness), self-direction | chance: 60
- B | get elected on a promise to cut the service charge, and deliver it | v: power, achievement | mark: kept your word | tags: commit:community | chance: 40
- R | stand up at the meeting and call the old guard out on their failures | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 48
- G | plant up the bare shared garden yourself, without waiting for permission | v: universalism (nature), self-direction | closed: approval: against the association's rules; backfire: told to dig it all up | chance: 65
- W>R | get the committee to throw a summer party in the courtyard | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 65
- U>G | survey every resident and plan a garden everyone can use | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 60
- B>W | trade your vote on the parking for a promise to fix the lifts | v: universalism, power | chance: 49
- R>U | demand, loudly, to see every invoice the committee has paid | v: universalism (fairness) | chance: 63
- G>B | rally the long-time residents to keep the newcomers off the committee | identity | v: power, tradition | closed: approval: shutting newcomers out breaks the association's rules; backfire: a formal complaint and a divided block | chance: 45
