works: {N} is voted in on a show of hands and a round of applause.
fails: The meetings turn into shouting matches, and nothing changes.
fails: {N} loses the vote by two, and {rival} smiles all the way to the lift.

== you cannot remember a word
tier: everyday
at: hospital
stages: elder
age: 62-110
calls: URG
tone: trouble
life: body, mind
horizon: moment
roles: friend, partner, child, a doctor
earth: halfway through a story, the word for the thing simply vanishes
times: Nearly every older person, most weeks; diary studies find tip-of-the-tongue moments grow more frequent with age (Burke and colleagues 1991)
gap: 0-1.5
scene: Halfway through a story at the table, the word is gone. {N} can see the thing, silver with a handle, but its name will not come.
scene W: {N} has always been the one who remembers birthdays and appointments. Losing a word feels like breaking a promise.
scene U: The word was there a second ago. {N} goes looking for it the way one looks for keys, retracing every step.
scene B: The table waits. {N} knows how it goes: the old ones who forget are the ones who get decided for.
scene R: The word is on the tip of {Ns} tongue, and {N} wants to bang the table until it falls out.
scene G: {Ns} mother lost her words like this, one by one, like leaves in autumn. {N} feels the same wind.
- W | book a memory check with the doctor, as the leaflet advises | v: security, conformity | chance: 75
- U | work round the gap, describing the thing until the word comes back | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B | laugh it off and steer the talk elsewhere before anyone notices | v: power (face) | chance: 80
- R | call it the whatsit and barrel on with the story, laughing | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 90
- G | let it go; words come and go now, as they did for your mother | v: tradition (accepting one's lot) | chance: 75
- W>U | do the crossword every morning without fail, to keep the mind sharp | habit | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 75
- U>B | teach yourself memory tricks so nobody ever sees you falter | v: power, achievement | mark: learned a skill | act: teach themselves memory tricks so nobody ever sees them falter | chance: 65
- B>R | book the trip you have saved for, while your mind is still clear | door | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 92
- R>G | sing the old songs out loud; those words never leave | habit | v: tradition, hedonism | chance: 90
- G>W | tell the family plainly, so everyone can plan for it together | v: benevolence, security | chance: 65
works: The word comes back an hour later, out in the garden, and {N} laughs out loud.
works: The doctor says it is ordinary forgetting, nothing more.
fails: It happens twice more that week, and {N} begins to dread conversations.
fails: {child} notices, and {N} sees the worry settle in {child}'s face.

