# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life events
# Retirement itself (the handover) is batch 1's "handing over your place"; this event is the new life after it.

== the first months of retirement
tier: life event
stages: mature elder
age: 55-80
per_year: 0 0 0 0 2 2
drivers: money+.4 health+.3 prosper+.3 era+.2
times: once, for most people who stop work for good, in the first year after the last day at work: most people in rich countries retire between 60 and 70, on average at about 64 for men and 63 for women across the OECD (Pensions at a Glance 2023); the engine lets in only people who retired within the year, so the yearly figure is per new retiree (Library 22:40)
likelier: money and a pension that pays enough, good health, a fixed pension age, a growing economy, a job that has changed past recognition
rarer: low money or no pension, poor health (which forces retirement instead), caring duties that already fill the days, work of one's own, a calling still loved
once: yes
domain: partner.3 community.3
rite: elder
tone: mixed
life: work, time
horizon: months
roles: partner, friend, former colleague, child, grandchild
earth: the leaving party is over, and Monday morning has no alarm
scene: The leaving card is on the mantelpiece and the alarm clock is unplugged. It is Monday morning, and nobody expects {N} anywhere.
scene W: For forty years {N} knew where to be at eight o'clock. Now the days need a shape, and {N} means to give them one.
scene U: Time at last for every book, course and question {N} put off for forty years. {N} makes a list, and the list is long.
scene B: {N} spent a career being someone people needed. The phone has gone quiet, and {N} has no intention of becoming nobody.
scene R: No boss, no commute, no meetings. {N} stands in the doorway in a dressing gown, grinning at the open week.
scene G: The garden needs digging, the grandchildren need collecting from school, and the season has turned. {N} lets it.
- W | draw up a weekly timetable: volunteering Mondays, the pool Wednesdays, family Sundays | habit | v: conformity, security | chance: 75
- U | enrol at the university for the degree you never had time to finish | identity | door | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 70
- B | go back as a paid consultant, on your own terms and at twice the rate | v: power, achievement | tags: commit:career; win:money+.05 | chance: 68
- R | buy an old camper van and just go | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | tech: car | chance: 76
- G | spend the mornings in the garden and the afternoons with family and friends | habit | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 79
- BG | take the lump sum, buy a smallholding and live off the land | binds | door | v: security, self-direction | grants: plot of land | chance: 48
- UG | walk the woods and rivers every day and become the local expert on birds | v: universalism (nature), self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- UR | turn the garage into a workshop and invent things all day long | habit | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 75
- WB | take the chair of a charity board and run it like your old department | identity | v: power, benevolence | chance: 62
- WR | volunteer at the refugee centre every day and fight its corner at the council | identity | binds | door | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 72
works: By spring, {Ns} days are fuller than they ever were at work.
works: {N} sleeps well, laughs more, and stops checking the old work emails.
fails: The days blur together, and {N} is still in a dressing gown at noon.
fails: {N} misses the work and the people more than expected, and the house is very quiet.

== your partner dies
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 45-110
per_year: 0 0 0 .002 .006 .04
drivers: harsh+.5 unrest+.3 money-.3 health-.3
times: once for most, rarely twice: per married person about 0.2 in 100 adults, 0.6 in 100 in midlife and 4 in 100 elders a year lose their partner (2 at 65-74, 5 at 75-84, 12 at 85 and over), women more often and earlier (D52, estimate from the SSA life table)
likelier: an older partner, a husband (men die younger), poverty, smoking, a long illness already in the house, a harsh place, war
rarer: a younger partner, money, good health and good health care
needs: partner
kills: partner
ends: partner
domain: partner1
rite: elder
tone: trouble
life: loss, partner
horizon: years
roles: partner, child, sibling, friend, a priest
earth: after decades together, your husband or wife dies
scene: {partner} died in the night. The other side of the bed is cold, and the whole house seems to be listening.
scene W: There are forms, a funeral, a notice in the paper and people to ring in the right order. {N} does each one, because {partner} would have.
scene U: {N} reads the death certificate again and again, the cause, the hour, as if the right reading could make sense of it.
scene B: Fifty years of sharing everything, and now the money, the house and every decision rest on {Ns} shoulders alone.
scene R: The grief hits {N} like weather, all at once, and {N} howls into {partner}'s coat in the hall.
scene G: The roses {partner} planted are in bud. They will flower without {partner}, and somehow, so will {N}.
- W | arrange the funeral exactly as you both agreed, and keep every custom | v: tradition, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 68
- U | read everything about grief and keep a journal of each day | habit | v: self-direction | chance: 68
- B | take charge of the money and the house at once, and decide everything alone | identity | v: self-direction, security | chance: 76
- R | let yourself fall apart, and cry wherever and whenever it comes | v: none clear (grief let out) | role: women | chance: 77
- G | keep their chair, their garden and their routines, as if they were still here | habit | v: tradition | mark: stayed home | chance: 68
- BR | sell up within the month and go somewhere neither of you ever went | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: moved away | chance: 48
- RG | walk out to the hills you both loved and scatter the ashes yourself | body: heavy | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 70
- UB | go through every one of their papers, looking for what they kept hidden | v: security, self-direction | chance: 64
- WG | let the family and old friends carry you through the first year | v: tradition, benevolence | act: let the family and old friends carry them through the first year | chance: 64
- WU | join a bereavement group and follow its course week by week | v: security, conformity | chance: 72
works: In time {N} can say {partner}'s name and smile.
works: The first year passes, and {N} is still standing, and not alone.
fails: {N} stops eating properly and barely leaves the house for months.
fails: The family drifts away after the funeral, and the silence is enormous.

== moving to a smaller home or into care
tier: life event
stages: mature elder
age: 55-110
per_year: 0 0 0 0 .01 .04
drivers: family-.4 health-.4 community-.3 prosper+.2
gap: 3-10
times: once or twice: about 1 in 100 a year in the late fifties and 4 in 100 elders downsize or move into care (D37, data and estimate: D37 counts any move, about 5 in 100 in midlife); moves into care rise steeply after 80
likelier: living alone, a divorce or a death in the house, renting, a fall or an illness, stairs, a house too big to keep up, family far away
rarer: a partner at home, family nearby, a close community, good health, an owned, paid-off house close to shops and doctors
rite: elder
moves: yes
tone: mixed
life: home
horizon: months
roles: child, sibling, neighbour, an estate agent, the care home staff
earth: the stairs have become too much, and the family house goes on the market
scene: Forty years of things in one house, and the new flat has two rooms. {N} stands in the attic with a roll of bin bags.
scene W: The family has agreed, the forms are signed, and the move is on the fourteenth. {N} labels every box with a date and a room.
scene U: {N} has compared eleven places: their staff, their fees, their inspection reports, and how far each is from the library.
scene B: The house is worth a fortune now. {N} means to get every penny of it and choose the next place without asking anyone's leave.
scene R: {N} cannot stand the thought of a beige room and a timetable, and the anger comes out at whoever is nearest.
scene G: This house is where {N} raised a family. Now it will raise someone else's. That is what houses are for.
- W | move on the date the family agreed, with every box labelled | v: conformity, security | chance: 70
- U | visit every place on the list and choose by the inspection reports | v: security, self-direction | chance: 70
- B | sell to the highest bidder and buy the best flat money can buy | v: power, security | chance: 78
- R | refuse to go, and dig in your heels for as long as you can stand | v: self-direction | chance: 50
- G | give the furniture to whoever will love it, and keep only the photographs | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: left home | chance: 70
- WUR | hold a farewell evening: a story for each room, then a dance in the empty one | v: hedonism, tradition | chance: 90
- WUG | choose a place in your own neighbourhood, with a garden, and move in slowly | v: tradition, security | chance: 70
- WBR | march into the care home office and get the best room in writing | v: power, security | chance: 58
- UBG | rent the house out, live on the income, and keep it in the family | binds | v: security, tradition | chance: 65
- BRG | sell up and go back to the village you were born in, whatever anyone says | identity | v: self-direction, tradition | mark: moved away | chance: 65
works: The new place is light and warm, and {N} is surprised to love it.
works: The move goes smoothly, and the neighbours are friendly from the first day.
fails: {N} cannot settle, and lies awake missing the old creaking stairs.
