== honoured for your life's work
tier: life event
stages: mature elder
age: 50-105
per_year: 0 0 0 0 .01 .02
drivers: community+.5 ties+.3 money+.2 prosper+.2 unrest-.3 fortune+.1
gap: 5-10
times: once or twice in later life: about 1 in 100 a year in midlife and 2 in 100 elders are publicly honoured for a lifetime's work (estimate scaled down from D22, which counts any recognition); the big public honours reach fewer than 1 in 1,000
likelier: a long career in one place, years of volunteering, a public role, a craft the town knows by name, wide ties, money that makes the work seen
rarer: a quiet life, frequent moves, work nobody sees, low money, unrest, discrimination
tone: joy
life: community, work
horizon: week
roles: mentor, colleague, friend, child, rival, the local paper
earth: a letter says you are to be honoured for a lifetime of work
scene: The letter is on heavy paper: {N} is to be honoured at a dinner for a lifetime's work. There will be a speech, and a photographer.
scene W: {N} reads the letter twice. It is an honour from the town {N} served for decades, and it should be received with dignity.
scene U: The citation gets two dates wrong and misnames the first project. {N} reaches for a pen.
scene B: At last the people who overlooked {N} for years will have to stand up and applaud.
scene R: {N} reads the letter and whoops out loud in the kitchen, then rings {friend} at once.
scene G: {N} thinks of {mentor}, who taught {N} the work, and of all the hands it passed through before and since.
